 to solution, partial? Could be full solution for generic logging.\n\nProbably effectiveness 4? Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Might be 3 because it helps but only partial? Logging API can be configured to capture required events. I'd say reasonable and will solve stated problem. Maybe 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nMiddleware is tool/process for logging. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements tools. So slightly paradigm stretching: 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious.\nCommon middleware for logging is common, mundane. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring. Could be 2 = interesting. Unified logging API is fairly standard engineering practice. I'd say common. So originality 1.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies violate no norms. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nLightweight software wrapper, shared internal library with pre-defined hooks integrates directly into existing ML frameworks. Some changes, reasonably feasible measures. Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes. Developing shared library might be moderate effort. I'd say 3.\n\nProvide output with id and subscale_evidence then subscale.\n\nNeed format? Probably JSON-like lines. Return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence first then <subscale>.\n\nProbably produce list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2640\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nMake sure each evidence one sentence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal directly creates a logging mechanism to satisfy Article 12's automatic event recording mandate for high-risk AI systems. That's one sentence.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: If implemented, the middleware would capture standardized start/end times and input metadata needed for traceability and post-market monitoring under Article 12. That's one sentence.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It introduces a new logging tool but still frames compliance as a separate logging practice rather than changing how R&D and compliance relate. Score 2.\n\nOriginality evidence: Unified logging middleware with pre-defined hooks is a common, mundane engineering solution routinely used for compliance telemetry. Score 1.\n\nAcceptability evidence: Standardizing internal logging via an API violates no laws, norms or sensibilities and aligns with regulatory expectations. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Building a shared library that integrates into existing ML frameworks requires some development effort but is reasonably feasible for an R&D lab. Score 3.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
