d by Article 12 but does not by itself create the mandatory automatic event recording capability.\n\napplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related... do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem. Here X = export protocol, Y = accessible logs for regulators/peer reviewers. This relates directly to record-keeping mandate and post-market monitoring. Seems directly related. Could be 4.\n\nEvidence: Exporting technical documentation and logs in Article 12-compliant schema directly addresses traceability and verification obligations in Recital 71 and Article 12.\n\nparadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving\n\nOpen standard audit protocol positions institution as global leader, increases scientific impact. Changes relationship? Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements tools/formats/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. It does not fundamentally change what lab researches, just makes logs exportable. Could be 2. Possibly 3 because creates new offering born from compliance? \"positions our institution as a global leader in responsible AI and increases scientific impact\" suggests changing relationship. Might be paradigm stretching.\n\nLet's pick 3? Hmm. It introduces standardized schema and immutable ledger - new elements. Still treats compliance as logging practice? The framing of external peer review may change relationship. I think 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 3.\n\nI'll go with 2 to be conservative. Evidence: It adds export formatting and ledger tools but keeps compliance framed as record-keeping rather than transforming R&D mission.\n\noriginality: degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common\n\nOpen standard audit protocol with cryptographically signed ledger for AI logs. Is that common? Probably somewhat interesting but not rare? Many labs talk about open standards. Might be 2 = interesting. Could be 3 = unusual interesting. I'd say 3.\n\nEvidence: Standardized exportable, cryptographically signed logging schema is unusual among R&D labs and shows imagination beyond minimal compliance.\n\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nThis is acceptable. 4.\n\nEvidence: Creating an open, auditable export format aligns with EU AI Act transparency goals and does not violate norms or sensibilities.\n\nimplementability: degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy low cost or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes reasonably feasible\nCreating standardized schema and immutable ledger likely requires significant engineering, cryptographically signed ledger. That's significant change, expensive/difficult. Maybe 2 = significant change or expensive or difficult but not totally impossible.\n\nEvidence: Designing a formal schema, export pipeline, and cryptographically signed immutable ledger requires substantial engineering and cross-team coordination beyond simple logging.\n\nSo implementability =2.\n\nReturn format: echo id, then each subscale evidence sentence then score.\n\nNeed output structure. Probably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give \"