ot totally impossible =2. Building unified layer across all pipelines is significant change. I'd say 2.\n\nacceptability (1-4): common strategies that violate no norms =4. This is standard compliance logging. Acceptable. Score 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem. Reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability =4? Will automate event recording and metadata capture, ensures uniform adherence to Article 12. Seems reasonable solves logging mandate. Maybe partial? It logs timestamps, data versions, user IDs. Article 12 requires logging events relevant for risk, post-market monitoring etc., plus specific for point 1(a) Annex III. Might be partial but overall effective. Could be 3 = contributes. I'd say 4: reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve main problem). Yes.\n\napplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem. Solves identified problem directly related =4. Do X to get Y and Y is part of stated problem. Yes.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale in order listed give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing id exactly. For each subscale give evidence first then score.\n\nProbably simple list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P1504\noriginality_evidence: ... \noriginality: 1\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence of evidence.\n\nProvide concise sentences.\n\nLet's craft evidences:\n\noriginality_evidence: A standardized logging library and API middleware for compliance is a common engineering response to audit requirements and lacks ingenuity or surprise.\noriginality: 1\n\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: The proposal adds a new logging tool but keeps R&D and compliance separate, treating Article 12 as a logging practice rather than changing the lab's research relationship to compliance.\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n\nimplementability_evidence: Integrating a unified middleware into all existing trai