s to ensuring compliance but does not itself create logs. Reasonable and will contribute to solution, partial solution.\nScore 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (helps but only partial)\n\napplicability: degree clearly applies to stated problem.\nSolves identified problem directly related? The problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Audit framework applies directly to meeting mandate via verification. That's directly related.\nScore 4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed format: For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably output JSON? Instruction says return rating... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably as lines.\n\nWe'll produce:\n\nid: P1602\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 1\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\nOriginality evidence: The checklist-based internal sign-off for logging compliance is a common, mundane quality-gate practice in regulated R&D labs.\nScore 1\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It treats compliance as standard logging practice and leaves the lab’s research workflow unchanged.\nScore 1\n\nAcceptability evidence: A mandatory internal audit and checklist sign-off violates no legal, social or business norms.\nScore 4\n\nImplementability evidence: Setting up a compliance office checklist and sign-off is an easy, low-cost procedural change.\nScore 4\n\nEffectiveness evidence: The audit will prevent non-compliant releases but does not itself build the required logging capabilities, so it only partially solves the mandate.\nScore 3\n\nApplicability evidence: Validating logging before testing directly addresses how the lab meets Article 12 record-keeping obligations.\nScore 4\n\nMake sure one sentence each.\n\nReturn.\n\n",
