tions, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners...)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way unchanged\n\nThis is introducing new tool but still treats compliance as logging practice. Slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's level 2.\n\nEvidence: The framework adds a standardized logging tool but keeps compliance as a separate logging practice rather than redefining R&D-compliance relationship.\n\nOriginality (1-4):\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nUnified logging framework is common. Centralized library for compliance logs is mundane. Score 1.\n\nEvidence: Centralized logging libraries for regulatory compliance are a common, mundane industry practice.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution...\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nWill it solve problem of meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D? It standardizes recording events throughout lifetime. Should ensure consistent legal compliance. Seems will solve. Reasonable and will solve stated problem. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Standardized automatic capture across projects directly addresses the mandate to technically allow automatic event recording for high-risk systems.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem\n3 = solves implied...\n2 = may have some benefit...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nDirectly related. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: The proposal explicitly targets the Article 12 requirement to automatically record events over system lifetime for high-risk AI.\n\nReturn rating echo id.\n\nNeed output format? Probably JSON with fields.\n\nInstruction: For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" (one sentence) first and then \"<subscale>\" (integer). So produce list.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2142\nacceptability_evidence: ...\nacceptability: 4\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence each.\n\nMake sure evidence is one sentence.\n\nProceed.",
