?\n\nThe stated problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Audit is about adapting R&D budgeting. That's adapt accordingly. So applies.\n\nI think applicability = 3? It solves an implied problem related to stated problem (resource planning). Could be 4? Does it solve identified problem? Identified problem is meeting logging mandate. Audit does not meet mandate, just measures cost. So not directly solve. It's implied.\n\nMaybe 2? May have some benefit within special situation... Hmm.\n\nProbably applicability = 3: solves implied problem of budgeting for compliance.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4): degree to which idea will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely to solve\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nAudit will not solve logging mandate; it just measures cost. It contributes to adaptation? Might contribute partially to solution (understanding costs). Could be 3 = contributes to solution. Or 2 = unlikely to solve problem.\n\nThe problem is meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Measuring overhead helps adapt but does not implement logging. Might be partial contribution.\n\nI think effectiveness = 3: reasonable and will contribute to solution (budget accuracy) but not solve logging itself.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nRunning benchmarks on current high-risk models to measure latency/storage. That's fairly easy? Requires some engineering time but feasible. Probably 3 or 4.\n\nAudit is formal analysis. Could be easy to implement at low cost? Might need some resources. I'd say 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. Or 4 = easy? Benchmarking is standard. Maybe 3.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nAudit of overhead is common business practice, acceptable. So 4.\n\nParadigm_relatedness (1-4):\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working is unchanged\n\nAudit is just measuring cost, still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new element? It's just analysis. Might be paradigm preserving: standard practice. Probably 1 = paradigm preserving. Could be 2 = slightly paradigm stretching introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. It introduces audit process. Hmm.\n\nThe description of 2: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. Audit is a process. Might be slightly stretching? Probably paradigm preserving because labs already do cost analysis. I'd give 1.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nCompute and storage overhead audit is common, mundane. So originality = 1.\n\nThus ratings:\napplicability_evidence: ... score 3\neffectiveness_evidence: ... score 3? Could be 2? Let's decide.\n\nAlternative interpretation: effectiveness could be 2 because