e, which is part of adapting R&D. Might be directly related.\n\nMaybe applicability 3: solves implied problem that is related - reduce manual record-keeping errors for compliance. I'd lean 4? The proposal does link logs to documentation. Might be directly related.\n\nLet's decide applicability = 3? Hmm.\n\nParadigm_relatedness: degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nAutomated documentation generator is tool integration, CI/CD step. Still treats compliance as logging practice, introduces new tool. That's slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2.\n\nOriginality: degree to which idea is rare but ingenious.\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious...\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nAutomated documentation generator from experiment tracking to regulatory templates is somewhat common industry practice (auto doc generation). Probably common/mundane. Maybe interesting? I'd say 2 = interesting, or 1 common. For AI labs, auto doc generation is becoming standard. Might be common. Let's give originality 2? Could be 1 common mundane. Hmm.\n\nReference class: set of responses experienced evaluator would expect AI R&D labs to give to this mandate. Expect automated tools? Maybe common. I'd rate 2 = interesting. Could be 1 common. Let's choose 2 = interesting (not totally mundane