1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nTiered storage for compliance logs is common IT practice. Not ingenious. Likely common/mundane. Could be interesting? Probably common, mundane. Level 1.\n\nEvidence: Tiered cold storage and automated deletion for regulatory logs is standard data lifecycle practice in regulated industries.\n\nScore 1.\n\neffectiveness (1-4): degree will solve problem.\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution\n2 = unreasonable or unlikely...\n1 = solves unrelated problem\n\nProblem: AI R&D lab must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce implementation resources.\n\nProposal addresses storage cost of logs, not automatic recording of events or traceability content. It helps manage compliance data but does not ensure logs capture required events per Art 12(2)(a-c). So contributes partially? It addresses retention/storage, which is part of record-keeping. Effectiveness maybe 3? It will contribute to solution of problem (manage storage) but not solve logging mandate itself. Could be partial solution. Might be reasonable and will contribute.\n\nScore 3? Or 2? The mandate requires technically allow automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, with specific content. Storage architecture does not provide logging capability. It assumes logs exist. So effectiveness at solving problem of meeting logging mandate is limited. It helps prevent storage crowding, which is a secondary concern. Might be effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution.\n\nLet's give 3.\n\nEvidence: The system improves long-term retention cost but does not create the required logging capabilities or content specified in Article 12.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree idea clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nIt does relate to record-keeping mandate. It addresses storage/accessibility requirement from Recital 71. That's directly related? The stated problem is how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Storage is part of meeting mandate. Could be 4? Solves identified problem that is directly related: managing compliance data storage to meet retention/accessibility. Might be 4.\n\nBut applicability definition: solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, Y is part of stated problem). The Y is cost-efficient retention. Is that part of stated problem? Stated problem is decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Managing storage is adaptation. Probably directly related.\n\nMaybe 3? Hmm.\n\nLet's interpret: effectiveness is about solving main problem, applicability about clear applies. I think applicability = 4 because it clearly applies to record-keeping requirement for retention and accessibility.\n\nEvidence: The proposal targets long-term retention and accessibility of logs and technical documentation explicitly required by Recital 71 and Article 12.\n\nScore 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive/difficult...\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nDedicated storage architecture with tiered storage and automated deletion is reasonably feasible for a lab with IT resources. Some changes. Probably 3.\n\nEvidence: Building a separate tiered storage system requires IT design and integration but uses mature cloud/on-prem tools and is reasonably feasible.\n\nScore 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\n2 = offends...\n1 = radically violates...\n\nThis is compliant, cost-efficient, no violation. Common strategy. Score 4.\n\nEvidence: Separating compliance data and automating deletion per legal limits aligns with EU AI Act requirements and standard data governance norms.\n\nScore 4.\n\nReturn rating.\n\nNeed format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nLet's output JSON? Probably list.\n\nProvide:\n\nid: P2880\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence: ...\nparadigm_relatedness: 2\n...\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nProceed.",
