adigm breaking would introduce new elements AND changes relationship OR addresses larger problem of what lab researches. This does change relationship but maybe not break? It says converts constraint into scientific opportunity. Might be paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (logging woven into science). That's level 3. Maybe level 4? Introduces new elements AND changes relationship... It introduces compressed logging formats... Hmm.\n\nDefinition: 4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes the relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what the lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into the science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n\nThe description for 3 explicitly mentions logging woven into science. That matches. So 3.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nAssign small cross-functional team to investigate... integrated into core infrastructure updates. That's some changes, reasonably feasible but research takes time/resources. Might be 3? Could be 2 because significant change expensive? Small team suggests moderate. I'd say 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, or politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\nProposal is research into logging compliance, acceptable. No violation. That's 4.\n\nNow need output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give \"<subscale>_evidence\" one sentence then \"<subscale>\" integer.\n\nOrder listed: applicability, effectiveness, originality, paradigm_relatedness, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide evidence sentence first then s