ghtly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements but still treats compliance as logging practice. Could be 2.\n\nEvidence: The framework adds logging tools and versioning but keeps R&D and compliance as separate logging practice rather than redefining research. Score 2.\n\nOriginality (1-4): degree rare but ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual interesting shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common mundane boring\n\nAutomated traceability framework for high-risk models with immutable ledger is fairly common. Probably common, mundane. Might be 1? Or 2 = interesting. Given typical proposals, automated logging middleware is common. I'd say 1 = common mundane boring.\n\nEvidence: Automated pipeline logging and immutable audit trails are common industry responses to AI regulation. Score 1.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>. Probably JSON? It says return rating... For each subscale give ... first and then score.\n\nProbably output as lines or JSON object with keys.\n\nI'll output JSON with id and fields.\n\nLet's produce:\n\nid: P2015\neffectiveness_evidence: ...\neffectiveness: 4\netc.\n\nMake sure one sentence evidence.\n\nEnsure order listed: effectiveness, applicability, acceptability, implementability, paradigm_relatedness, originality\n\nReturn rating.\n\nLet's craft concise one-sentence evidence.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Automating event logging and versioning from data curation through deployment directly satisfies Article 12's automatic record-keeping mandate. Score 4.\n\nApplicability evidence: The proposal addresses the identified problem of meeting mandatory logging for high-risk AI systems in R&D. Score 4.\n\nAcceptability evidence: An immutable audit trail via middleware is a legally compliant, socially neutral engineering practice. Score 4.\n\nImplementability evidence: Integrating a standardized software layer across training and inference pipelines re