arger problem of what lab researches? It says turns burden into publication opportunity regarding AI transparency and self-documenting systems. That is changing relationship, maybe not breaking. I'd say 3.\n\nWorkability acceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially legally politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Using generative AI to synthesize compliance reports required by Act. Is that acceptable? Might be questionable: regulators may require accurate documentation; using generative AI might introduce hallucination risk, potentially non-compliant. Could offend sensibilities somewhat? Probably somewhat uncommon but does not offend sensibilities. Might be 3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities. Or 4 common strategies? Automated documentation is common, using generative AI is newer. I'd say 3.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nAllocate small team to develop AI agents that translate low-level event data into human-readable compliance reports. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Developing generative AI agents is significant change, expensive or difficult but not totally impossible. Could be 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible. Or 3 = some changes, reasonably feasible. The proposal says allocate small team, research thread. Might be research not immediate implementation. Since approval means committing scarce implementation resources to pursue a proposal, not funding to explore whether idea might someday work. This is research into... That seems like exploration, not guaranteed implementation. Implementability maybe 2? Significant change expensive/difficult. I'd give 2 or 3. Hmm.\n\nInstrument says implementability 4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible