ble ledger for specific Annex III logging is... Probably interesting but not highly original? Might be unusual? Could be 2 = interesting. Maybe 3? Let's think: experienced evaluator would expect AI labs to give responses like build logging tool. Specialized audit module with immutable ledger tracking reference DB identifiers etc. Is that common? Logging tools are common. Immutable ledger is somewhat interesting. Probably originality 2 = interesting. Might be 3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal is fairly specific to mandates 3(a)-(d). That's basically compliance mapping. Not ingenious. I'd say 2 interesting.\n\nImplementability (1-4): degree can be easily implemented.\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures\n2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult, but not totally impossible\n1 = totally infeasible\n\nBuilding high-precision immutable ledger with digital signatures and restricted access is significant change, expensive/difficult but not impossible. Probably 2.\n\nEvidence: Requires building custom immutable ledger with digital signatures and role-based access for Annex III 1(a) systems.\n\nAcceptability (1-4): degree socially legally politically acceptable.\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon or unusual strategies that do not offend sensibilities\n2 = offends sensibilities somewhat but not totally unacceptable\n1 = radically violates laws...\n\nImmutable ledger restricted to audit roles is acceptable. Probably 4 = common strategies that violate no norms. Or 3? Restricting access to audit roles is normal. So 4.\n\nApplicability (1-4): degree clearly applies to stated problem.\n4 = solves identified problem directly related\n3 = solves implied problem related\n2 = may have some benefit within special situation...\n1 = intervention not stated...\n\nProposal directly addresses logging mandate for Annex III 1(a). So 4.\n\nEffec