t funds cost. It contributes to solution? It helps lab afford compliance but does not meet logging requirement itself. So effectiveness 3 = reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps, but only partial). Could be 2 unreasonable? It contributes financially. Probably 3.\n\nimplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Allocating percentage budget and central fund for credits. Some changes, reasonably feasible. Score 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nacceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies violate no norms. Score 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each before number.\n\nOutput format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence then <subscale>.\n\nProbably JSON? It says return rating... For each subscale ... give \"<subscale>_evidence\" first and then \"<subscale>\". Probably lines.\n\nProvide:\n\nid: P1619\noriginality_evidence: ...\noriginality: 1\n...\n\nLet's ensure order listed: originality, paradigm_relatedness, applicability, effectiveness, implementability, acceptability\n\nProvide concise one sentence evidence.\n\nOriginality evidence: The funding-offset program is a routine internal budgeting practice for compliance overhead and is common in regulated labs. Score 1.\n\nParadigm_relatedness evidence: It adds a financial allocation process but leaves R&D work and logging obligations unchanged as separate compliance tasks. Score 2.\n\nApplicability evidence: It directly addresses the implied budget impact of meeting Article 12 logging requirements without changing how logs are created. Score 3? Need one sentence.\n\nEffectiveness evidence: Funding does not create logs or change technical implementation, so it only eases resource constraints rather than solving traceability. Score 3? Wait if effectiveness is will solve problem. It contributes partially. Could be 3.\n\nImplementability evidence: Setting aside a budget perce