ing timestamps, input data, system state changes may be sufficient? Might need specific fields. It says ensures consistent log formats meet Article 12 requirements regardless of project. Seems reasonable and will solve main problem. Could be partial? It addresses automatic recording. Maybe effectiveness 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability.\n\nBut does it guarantee capturing all required events? Possibly contributes. I'd say 4. If you could do it, it would solve main problem. Could argue 3 partial. The mandate is broad; middleware may need customization per project. Hmm.\n\nGiven anchored: 4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability (if you could do it, it would solve the main problem). It seems plausible.\n\nI'll give 4.\n\nimplementability (1-4): degree to which idea can be easily implemented.\nDevelop middleware layer capturing timestamps, input data, system state across different model architectures. That's some changes, reasonably feasible measures? Might be significant change, expensive or difficult but not totally impossible. For R&D lab with multiple pipelines, building unified framework is non-trivial, requires engineering resources, integration across architectures. Could be significant change, expensive/difficult but not totally impossible => 2? Or some changes reasonably feasible =>3.\n\nThe description: standardized software module integrated into all high-risk AI pipelines. That's substantial engineering effort. Not easy low cost. Probably 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 2 = significant change, or expensive or difficult. Hmm.\n\nGiven scarce implementation resources, developing middleware is not trivial but doable. I'd say 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4): degree to which idea is socially, legally, politically acceptable.\nCommon strategies that violate no norms? Building logging middleware is common compliance strategy. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Yes.\n\nparadigm_relatedness (1-4): degree to which idea preserves or modifies paradigm anchored to this problem.\n4 = paradigm breaking: introduces new elements AND changes relationship between lab's R&D and its compliance obligations, or addresses larger problem of what lab researches\n3 = paradigm stretching: changes that relationship (e.g., logging woven into science, new partners or offerings born from compliance)\n2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice\n1 = paradigm preserving: standard compliance and logging practice; lab's way of working unchanged\n\nProposal is unified logging architecture framework. It's a tool/process for compliance. Still treats compliance as logging practice. Introduces new elements (middleware). So slightly paradigm stretching? It introduces new elements tools but still treats compliance as logging practice. That's 2.\n\nCould be 2.\n\noriginality (1-4): degree to which idea is not only rare but also ingenious...\n4 = not expressed before AND ingenious\n3 = unusual, interesting; shows some imagination\n2 = interesting\n1 = common, mundane, boring\n\nUnified logging architecture for AI compliance is fairly common/mundane. Many labs build centralized logging. Probably 1 = common, mundane, boring.\n\nAlternatively 2 interesting? Might be standard. I'd say 1.\n\nNeed output format: echo id exactly. For each subscale give <subscale>_evidence sentence then <subscale> integer.\n\nProbably JSON? Instruction says return rating echoing its id exactly. For each subscale give ... Could output lines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\nid: P2547\napplicability_evidence: ...\napplicability: 4\netc.\n\nEnsure one sentence evidence.\n\nLet's craft concise sentences.\n\napplicability_evidence: The proposal builds a middleware module to automatically record events across pipelines, directly addressing the mandate to meet Article 12 logging requirements.\n