e unusual? I'd say interesting not rare. Maybe 2.\n\nEvidence: Creating synthetic failures to test risk logs is a familiar validation approach in safety engineering rather than an ingenious surprise.\n\nScore 2.\n\nimplementability (1-4):\n4 easy low cost\n3 some changes, reasonably feasible\n2 significant change, expensive/difficult but not totally impossible\n1 totally infeasible\n\nSimulation suite requires building specialized testing environment. Some changes, reasonably feasible? Could be significant change. Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures.\n\nEvidence: Building a synthetic-failure testbed requires engineering effort and data but uses existing logging infrastructure.\n\nScore 3.\n\nacceptability (1-4):\n4 common strategies violate no norms\n3 somewhat uncommon but do not offend sensibilities\n2 offends sensibilities somewhat\n1 radically violates\n\nAcceptable. 4 = common strategies that violate no norms.\n\nEvidence: Proactively testing compliance logs aligns with regulatory expectations and professional norms.\n\nScore 4.\n\neffectiveness (1-4):\n4 reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\n3 reasonable and will contribute to solution (partial)\n2 unreasonable unlikely to solve\n1 solves unrelated problem\n\nStated problem: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Approval means committing scarce resources. This validates logs for risk-identification, which is part of mandate but does not itself implement logging. It contributes? Might help ensure compliance but doesn't provide logging. Effectiveness maybe 3 = contributes to solution partial.\n\nEvidence: Validating that logs flag Article 79 risks helps ensure traceability but does not by itself create the required automatic logging capability.\n\nScore 3.\n\napplicability (1-4):\n4 solves identified problem directly related\n3 solves implied problem related\n2 may have some benefit within special situation\n1 intervention not stated..