g. Maybe 1 = common mundane. Hmm.\n\nStandard practice to centralize logging via SDK. Probably common, mundane. Let's pick 2? The instrument says common, mundane, boring =1. Shared library for compliance is fairly standard engineering solution. I'd say 1 common mundane. Could be 2 interesting. Let's go with 2? Let's decide 2 = interesting. Hmm.\n\nThe proposal is not ingenious. It's a standard wrapper. Probably originality 2? Might be 1. Let's pick 1 common mundane boring.\n\nAcceptability (1-4):\n4 = common strategies that violate no norms\n3 = somewhat uncommon...\nProposal is socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategy. So 4.\n\nImplementability (1-4):\n4 = easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes\n3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible\nWrapper around existing telemetry tools to automatically capture... Might require development effort across diverse model architectures. Probably some changes, reasonably feasible. Not trivially easy. Could be 3.\n\nEvidence: requires building shared library and integration across architectures, which is feasible but not trivial low-cost.\n\nEffectiveness (1-4):\n4 = reasonable and will solve stated problem without regard for workability\nWill it solve logging mandate? It provides standardized API for recording event types required by Article 12. Captures timestamps, input references, session IDs. For high-risk systems in Annex III point 1(a) need specific items: period of use, reference database, input data matched, identification of natural persons. Proposal mentions timestamps, input references, session IDs. Might partially cover. It ensures compliance via single dependency rather than fragmented manual efforts. Seems reasonable and will solve main problem of meeting logging mandate. Probably 4.\n\nApplicability (1-4):\n4 = solves an identified problem that is directly related to stated problem (do X to get Y, and Y is part of stated problem)\nIt directly addresses how to meet logging mandate. So 4.