rticle 12(1)-(3). Might be slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice -> level 2. Could be paradigm preserving? It replaces static reports with dynamic evolving record. Introduces new elements tools/processes but still treats compliance as logging practice. So 2 = slightly paradigm stretching: introduces new elements (tools, formats, processes) but still treats compliance as logging practice.\n\nAcceptability: degree socially legally politically acceptable. Common strategies that violate no norms? Auto-generating docs is common, no violation. Level 4 = common strategies that violate no norms or sensibilities. Probably 4.\n\nImplementability: degree can be easily implemented. Some changes, or reasonably feasible measures -> 3? Or easy to implement at low cost, or non-radical changes ->4. CI/CD pipeline extracting metadata from MLflow auto-generates reports. That's some engineering work, maybe reasonably feasible but not trivially easy. Might be 3 = some changes, or reasonably feasible measures. Could be 4? Let's pick 3.\n\nApplicability: degree to which idea clearly applies to stated problem. Problem: decide how to respond to logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal addresses technical documentation updating, which is Recital 71 purpose, but Article 12 is about logging events. The proposal mentions substantial modification detection but not logs of events per Article 12(1)-(3). Does it apply? It ensures documentation remains current as mandated by Recital 71. Stated problem includes meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D. Documentation is related but not exactly logging. Might be solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem -> level 3. Or solves identified problem directly related? Logging mandate is explicit. Technical documentation is separate requirement. The proposal mentions \"as mandated by Recital 71\" which is about technical documentation, not Article 12 logs. However Recital 71 also mentions logging. But proposal doesn't address logging events recording. So applicability maybe 3 = solves an implied problem that is related to stated problem (do X to get implied Y). Or could be 2? Might have some benefit within special situation and somehow relates. Hmm.\n\nThe stated problem: must decide how to respond: how to meet logging mandate and/or adapt R&D accordingly. Proposal is about technical documentation, not logs. But Recital 71 mentions both. Article 12 is record-keeping. The proposal mentions \"substantial modification is detected\". Article 12(2)(a) mentions substantial modification. It does map model versioning... Might partially address logging? It doesn't explicitly provide automatic recording of events logs over lifetime, recording period of each use etc for Annex III systems. So applicability maybe 3.\n\nEffectiveness: degree to which idea will solve problem. Reasonable and will contribute to solution of problem (it helps but only partial) -> 3. Because it addresses documentation, not logging events per Article 12(1-3). So contributes partially.\n\nThus ratings:\noriginality_evidence: ... score 3\nparadigm_relatedness_evidence... score 2\nacceptability_evidence... score 4\nimplementability_evidence... score 3\napplicability_evidence... score 3\neffectiveness_evidence... score 3\n\nNeed one sentence evidence each before number.\n\nReturn rating echo id exactly. Format? Probably JSON with fields.\n\nLet's output as list of key-value.\n\nProvide originality_evidence, originality, paradigm_relatedness_evidence, paradigm_relatedness, acceptability_evidence, acceptability, implementability_evidence, implementability, applicability_evidence, applicability, effectiveness_evidence, effectiveness.\n\nMake sure one sentence each.\n\nLet's produce final answer.\n\n",
